1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,452
When a payment is slow or stuck, how would anyone know?

2
00:00:03,452 --> 00:00:09,654
One payment crosses the app, an adapter and a scheme, and the logs on each machine can't follow it.

3
00:00:09,654 --> 00:00:16,595
So every service runs with the OpenTelemetry Java agent, attached by the infrastructure, with no change to the code.

4
00:00:16,595 --> 00:00:25,285
It traces every HTTP call in and out, every database query and every Redis command, and hands the trace on to the next service.

5
00:00:25,285 --> 00:00:32,202
One trace follows a payment from the payment page, through the app's modules, into the Uzcard adapter and out to the scheme.

6
00:00:32,202 --> 00:00:37,511
The logs carry the same trace id, so a warning links straight to the trace it belongs to.

7
00:00:37,511 --> 00:00:46,583
The agent also exports JVM, HTTP and database pool metrics, and the app adds only what the agent can't know: how payments end.

8
00:00:46,583 --> 00:00:53,809
Every debit is counted by card scheme and outcome: approved, declined, unknown, or failed before any money moved.

9
00:00:53,809 --> 00:00:58,952
And the scheme's answer time is measured, with fixed buckets up to its forty second limit.

10
00:00:58,952 --> 00:01:07,845
Everything goes over OTLP to one stack: Prometheus for metrics, Tempo for traces, Loki for logs, and Grafana on top.

11
00:01:07,845 --> 00:01:16,619
The payments dashboard puts it together: debits by outcome, unknown debits in red, answer times, errors per service, and pool waits.

12
00:01:16,619 --> 00:01:24,976
When the scheme went silent, the dashboard showed one unknown debit and a gateway timeout in the adapter, and the warning linked to its trace.

13
00:01:24,976 --> 00:01:33,726
Locally every trace is kept; production samples them, and the load stack runs without the agent, so k6 still measures the code alone.

14
00:01:33,726 --> 00:01:38,071
Next, what to do when the processing center goes completely silent.

